#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/id #uuid "76b310bf-b592-541c-82e8-93d72bc688f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21778" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/284b6c87b050df7b5663c21dcf357b1f2fa66a86f6c28604ab6681267394ada1.png)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/f6f630f08ab3b335d43d12fa90c45df8fad0c73b83df793f43958e1ada7d199c.png)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)} & =\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "8587baf6-8f11-56eb-8afa-dd385c0aeaeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21779" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2e180f0d1ea06cd71caafeafb99628fdc8560253293ee5e9375dc84d5b735a64.png)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y & =p(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+5{y}_{p}^{′}-6{y}_{p} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p',$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})}^{′}+5({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})-6({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}) & =p(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}+36{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\,+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+5{e}^{x}-6{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(36{e}^{-6x}-30{e}^{-6x}-6{e}^{-6x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}=p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6615" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20cb5a33-c529-52e1-b3d0-21f2d46432f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360287} {:answer/id #uuid "82c36687-692e-5037-86ea-8267bb1cda53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-5x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360288} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7f0a67-3ffc-58e2-836a-f165d7c05a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360289} {:answer/id #uuid "aca675c8-10ff-5ce1-884a-f037cbc52733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd7f0a67-3ffc-58e2-836a-f165d7c05a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360289} :answer-field/id #uuid "064b03b9-803f-5e6f-9b5e-750a93298d91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360286} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52489bc6-a9db-594b-8778-e8a3742f49b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360292} {:answer/id #uuid "36439b46-a787-574e-a810-c4accdf69b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-5x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360293} {:answer/id #uuid "b43e79b0-bfb7-565a-8c94-aa825a81b9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360294} {:answer/id #uuid "ec27bd6d-c436-57d3-837c-b1ce2299032c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52489bc6-a9db-594b-8778-e8a3742f49b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360292} :answer-field/id #uuid "8546fe99-37a1-5d6c-9fe4-cade55e9b30e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360291} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6d0eab-609e-53b1-946d-f738c1ebd3f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360297} {:answer/id #uuid "4e800c30-1f19-500e-bc5c-f54741a5654c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}={h}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360298} {:answer/id #uuid "f97c188e-9c13-5f5b-aecd-dabbb00c854d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}={h}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360299} {:answer/id #uuid "990e5cba-3b32-5896-b45c-7eaef72af243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990e5cba-3b32-5896-b45c-7eaef72af243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360300} :answer-field/id #uuid "7928e6bd-3b9c-50bf-ad7a-85a7ab8cfb89" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ae91b97-502d-5bff-ae2a-bc21f5a4e0d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337736" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+20y=h(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-5x}:$According to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $h(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+20y & =h(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+9{y}_{p}^{′}+20{y}_{p} & =h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′})}^{′}+9({u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′})+20({u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =h(x) \\\\ {(-4{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x})}^{′}-36{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-45{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}+20{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+20{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x} & =h(x) \\\\ -4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}+25{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}-36{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-45{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}+20{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+20{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x} & =h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{(16{e}^{-4x}-36{e}^{-4x}+20{e}^{-4x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(25{e}^{-5x}-45{e}^{-5x}+20{e}^{-5x})}_{⏟}}_{0}-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48b7dd37-7cec-5427-a582-84709e120a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360303} {:answer/id #uuid "9b68cca0-d219-5c6e-9e27-f5f25f5f0fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360304} {:answer/id #uuid "523503bd-40bd-5e12-9f91-3b4b373540d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360305} {:answer/id #uuid "b5524f9a-8e23-5b4c-abbc-72facc9244f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b7dd37-7cec-5427-a582-84709e120a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360303} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c216d07-971d-5200-8cd4-1659d8b9c656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87fb5b8-8063-5db1-bbfd-c2ab4b166ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360308} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5603e9-1f99-52f3-b412-13a65e5e40dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360309} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4922d3-82f0-5a59-8ed0-e45a523b636a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360310} {:answer/id #uuid "5854ccdf-9fb6-59ba-90c5-646c0a3ecd0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5854ccdf-9fb6-59ba-90c5-646c0a3ecd0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360311} :answer-field/id #uuid "dec0b793-0eb0-54ca-9f2f-733df99690ba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4bd364b-9054-54ea-8aa4-482793bd2f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360313} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0d428e-e67f-5a6c-aec4-9c804e547d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360314} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4af752-7e76-5f1d-95e7-0b877f4584a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360315} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd9ea9f-f152-5d31-b5f4-85beee027709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b4af752-7e76-5f1d-95e7-0b877f4584a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360315} :answer-field/id #uuid "71179e34-7c7f-5cae-a3c9-dd98c35ced32" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "738e3bfd-8ec4-5909-8a10-7256f431f968" :question/math-academy-id "q-337737" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}:$According to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+7{y}_{p}^{′}+12{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})}^{′}+7({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})+12({u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {(-3{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x})}^{′}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x) \\\\ -3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-21{e}^{-3x}+12{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(16{e}^{-4x}-28{e}^{-4x}+12{e}^{-4x})}_{⏟}}_{0}-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67a290f-430d-5c97-b5c3-c7e772da30a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360319} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0dbf46-55b3-50d9-87cd-5175dbe0506a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360320} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb8e142-878c-580c-990e-c509d4b84ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360321} {:answer/id #uuid "18784833-0bfc-5b6d-ab3d-fcc66582304c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb8e142-878c-580c-990e-c509d4b84ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360321} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d742182-91fb-5f4e-b1b1-013b758a6920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360318} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf0df23-5273-5117-8bd3-024e8bee9ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360324} {:answer/id #uuid "7252a703-1998-5754-a4a7-616ea39dc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360325} {:answer/id #uuid "976d4d8c-4944-528e-87df-a8f5b24e93a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360326} {:answer/id #uuid "26eca237-6cd9-51f9-8f76-45626ce52adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7252a703-1998-5754-a4a7-616ea39dc960" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360325} :answer-field/id #uuid "b367acad-7e3d-5384-b0c1-91054748e4fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4273c6cc-2deb-54f3-b563-fd94a1589eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360329} {:answer/id #uuid "d46e0180-b874-5c24-936f-53271142bf57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360330} {:answer/id #uuid "e694c571-59ff-58b6-8e0f-d2244e3e2a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4273c6cc-2deb-54f3-b563-fd94a1589eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360329} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d2e665-c264-5917-8584-9056f9ddbe4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbf0fbd-a96f-561b-b971-f62151ea3b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360333} {:answer/id #uuid "ff41373e-e122-506d-9c48-2bbbdb33e59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360334} {:answer/id #uuid "279bb08c-7663-52fc-85bc-60058c41e617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360335} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8d3e26-1d38-51bf-b78b-0ece1d43b123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279bb08c-7663-52fc-85bc-60058c41e617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360335} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5f1c5a-3d10-5048-9ac7-9174321b4b5b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "126af949-4137-5e8c-b099-8e339ab02e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337938" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y={e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{3x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} & =2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f66acf8e-776c-55bf-b2fd-4e997dae51df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360339} {:answer/id #uuid "401b1437-7742-5ee3-9e7c-61a059776a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360340} {:answer/id #uuid "b17a3595-c281-52ce-8591-c8ff916d8392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360341} {:answer/id #uuid "2b350163-800b-5de5-bbed-8a24a8609187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "401b1437-7742-5ee3-9e7c-61a059776a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360340} :answer-field/id #uuid "107a6492-2d3a-5a46-a930-50d06c41f3cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f46d75f-23c8-50ed-96bb-6252143d73f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360344} {:answer/id #uuid "8bae86ab-0785-596d-b61e-78043c7f2a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360345} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac7a2dc-7ad6-53bb-aff3-fa70a6bd2eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360346} {:answer/id #uuid "84337cb3-fadd-5ef9-ad0b-be88ccb74641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84337cb3-fadd-5ef9-ad0b-be88ccb74641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360347} :answer-field/id #uuid "58d3a4d0-5c4e-5d6f-8f92-96c7f4881d15" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ef358a3-d256-5882-8a0a-4795835ae625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360349} {:answer/id #uuid "f658039a-1220-54e7-806b-425c0088d1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360350} {:answer/id #uuid "0d53bf26-66ea-5d90-9fd8-a3940edfb1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f658039a-1220-54e7-806b-425c0088d1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d66fa2d-b6f1-506e-bb7c-57b1e6cdba7e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e193d0b-be07-5a91-a02a-2d7c029d118f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360353} {:answer/id #uuid "64a19f4a-8feb-5c87-a385-ad94a7562d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360354} {:answer/id #uuid "d47d4944-92fb-58ba-a6fb-fce232b610b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360355} {:answer/id #uuid "10bae5ba-7142-5450-8622-f00ee983eeb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e193d0b-be07-5a91-a02a-2d7c029d118f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360353} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0864e5a-6cfa-5392-9ada-d2619b96190d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c18ea98c-e252-5307-934c-561722318fe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-337942" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\cancel{2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}-\\cancel{2{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ddb852-8381-5439-a972-2f3ef60797b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360359} {:answer/id #uuid "dd67de29-6dd8-515e-aff3-bb7ca11f2c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360360} {:answer/id #uuid "db1d99cc-00b0-5c24-a225-71a15a23e6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360361} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5383fe-20a9-5240-9c94-0a1bb0f1b563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360362} {:answer/id #uuid "fefccb76-a34f-59f0-a1d9-ca59608c12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fefccb76-a34f-59f0-a1d9-ca59608c12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360363} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b6665eb-23df-5a65-a0e6-02b101c676a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1135cd4b-ca21-5439-9cb1-6a63dd32c83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338337" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-3x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}){e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 6{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd777955-5496-54c9-a6ed-3cc33c655a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360366} {:answer/id #uuid "835263ab-6bae-58d7-85de-0db83a3808be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360367} {:answer/id #uuid "164ab6e6-ce54-58c3-801a-312372e06186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360368} {:answer/id #uuid "58445931-a984-57ff-835f-30d353660bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360369} {:answer/id #uuid "c2285890-1e24-5620-be8f-a1fcf3be71d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2285890-1e24-5620-be8f-a1fcf3be71d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360370} :answer-field/id #uuid "96369a2d-26af-5af4-9579-44f35598d10f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf0b872-6799-5596-9af5-6e6078cbb1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-338348" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x})^{′}-({e}^{4x})^{′}({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})(-4{e}^{-4x})-(4{e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =-4-4 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{4x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-4x})}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-4x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}){e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 8{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int 2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e595428d-ef4d-5033-84dc-d5a232a9fbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360373} {:answer/id #uuid "1eba6377-981f-5ef2-85fe-0e759b1115a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360374} {:answer/id #uuid "090852c9-c0a8-5b31-ab4f-b50a6b3eb895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360375} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe6cd1-fe0e-5353-92c7-b33a6cbdce7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360376} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c53ee6-91c8-5ffa-a784-deb7e8b4f2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e595428d-ef4d-5033-84dc-d5a232a9fbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360373} :answer-field/id #uuid "66efd36d-7ac5-5e12-a788-16a77adc6bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acd1195a-dbcf-5149-b1a9-55f5d1f22df5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338349" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b82ce0-2d8f-5d6c-8e1d-b99df9c8c007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360380} {:answer/id #uuid "491ca38f-18f5-557b-8541-5f37c8f142c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360381} {:answer/id #uuid "91249d03-1380-5a99-bf5a-0847fa1ea1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360382} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4ab639-6bd4-5a55-854e-67b1387c7140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360383} {:answer/id #uuid "c62ac8ac-c5ab-5a94-86f4-ce1766da4d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed4ab639-6bd4-5a55-854e-67b1387c7140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360383} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecb9909-6f93-59d4-a9f7-51d6057651d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48baa0b8-dda4-5361-b9d7-50f764eba06c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338433" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}){e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbeecbd9-5f77-5be0-bbcb-f94a78c0c14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360387} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbbebcc-c01d-5f54-b264-4a68cf11901c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360388} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8b2143-b0d7-5bcb-ae4b-2a469f13baeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360389} {:answer/id #uuid "213d25ad-40bd-5a75-8fd3-0d774b0dc7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360390} {:answer/id #uuid "e958580d-5630-5574-ad9e-f1148efe2e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e958580d-5630-5574-ad9e-f1148efe2e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360391} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3d24891-58f4-5e62-88fb-780f08f31bf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16aa4a71-1d22-59d3-bba7-4dc54427fd4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338434" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}){e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 5{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}]}
